<%# DEFECT 3's lab page — an @-time stamp and the script that localises it.

    The server renders the stamp in the APP timezone with the flag hidden, because
    it cannot know where the reader sits. studio/_at_time_script is the client half
    that re-stamps it to the VIEWER's clock and reveals a country flag when the
    viewer's IANA zone is outside the US.

    That split is why this page is the sharpest test in the lane. The server's
    output is the SAME bytes whether the script runs or not, so every assertion a
    Rails view test can make passes on a page whose client half is dead — which is
    exactly how the phantom-element defect shipped, with a guard test
    (`assert_includes html, "__atTimeFmt"`) that passed on the broken page because
    the token survived inside the text the phantom element had swallowed.

    A fixed epoch, not Time.current: the spec asserts an exact wall-clock string,
    so the input may not drift between runs. 2026-03-14 12:34:00 UTC. %>
<% stamp_time = Time.at(1_773_491_640).utc %>

<p>
  Deployed <%= at_time_tag(stamp_time, now: stamp_time) %>
</p>

<%= render "studio/at_time_script" %>
